---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="How Does the CRS Score Work? | AfriMigrate"
  description="A plain-language breakdown of Canada's Comprehensive Ranking System (CRS): every scoring factor explained, and how to raise your score."
>
  <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blog">Blog</a> / How CRS Score Works</div>
  <div class="eyebrow">Guide · Canada</div>
  <h1>How Does the CRS Score Work?</h1>
  <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
    The Comprehensive Ranking System scores every Express Entry candidate out of 1,200 points. Here's exactly what's being measured, and why.
  </p>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Core Factors</div>
    <h2>The first 500 points</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width: 700px; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      Your age, education, language ability and Canadian work experience make up your "core human capital" score — worth up to 500 points if you're single, or split across you and your spouse if you have one.
    </p>
    <ul style="list-style: disc; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: var(--ink2); font-weight: 300; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem; max-width: 700px;">
      <li><strong style="color: var(--ink);">Age:</strong> peaks between 20–29, then declines — there's no way around this one except applying sooner rather than later.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--ink);">Education:</strong> a Master's degree scores meaningfully higher than a Bachelor's — an ECA is required to prove your foreign credential's Canadian equivalency.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--ink);">Language:</strong> the single most improvable factor. Moving from CLB 7 to CLB 9 across all four abilities can add well over 100 points.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--ink);">Work experience:</strong> scored in bands (1, 2–3, 4–5, 6+ years) — more years help, but with diminishing returns after 3.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Skill Transferability</div>
    <h2>The 100-point bonus most people miss</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width: 700px;">
      Combinations of education + language, or education + Canadian work experience, unlock additional "transferability" points. A strong language score doesn't just count once — it compounds with your other factors here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Additional Points</div>
    <h2>The 600-point lever</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width: 700px;">
      A provincial nomination adds 600 points outright — more than most people can gain from every other factor combined. If your core score isn't clearing recent draw cut-offs, this is almost always the more efficient path.
    </p>
    <a href="/canada/pnp" class="btn-primary" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">Check Provincial Nominee Programs →</a>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Try It Yourself</div>
    <h2>Calculate your actual score</h2>
    <a href="/canada/crs-calculator" class="btn-primary" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">Calculate my CRS score →</a>
  </section>
</Layout>
